WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.420
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:11.220
Today we are going take a collection of photos
like these, and magically, create a video

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.829
where we can fly through these photos.

00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:20.960
And it gets better, we will be able to do
it quickly, and, get this, no AI is required.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.770
So, how is this even possible?

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.920
Especially that the input is only a handful
of photos.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:34.210
Well, typically, we give it to a learning
algorithm and ask it to synthesize a photorealistic

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.320
video where we fly through the scene as we
please.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.870
Of course, that sounds impossible.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:47.480
Especially that some information is given
about the scene, but this is really not much.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.570
And as you see, this is not impossible at
all - through the power of learning-based

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:58.899
techniques, this previous AI is already capable
of pulling off this amazing trick.

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:03.840
And today, I am going to show you that through
this incredible new paper, that something

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.380
like this can even be done at home, on our
own machines.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.770
Now, the previously showcased technique, and
its predecessors are building on gathering

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:18.369
training data and training a neural network
to pull this off.

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:23.119
Here you see the training process of one of
them compared to the reference results.

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:28.869
Well, it looks like we need to be really patient
as this process is quite lengthy, and for

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:33.970
the majority of the time, we don’t get any
usable results for nearly a day into this

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:34.970
process.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:39.810
Now, hold on to your papers, because here
comes the twist.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.380
What is the twist?

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.420
Well, these are not reference results.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.500
No-no.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.750
These are the results from the new technique.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.790
Yes, you heard it right.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:56.150
It doesn’t require a neural network, and
thus trains so quickly, that it almost immediately

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:02.250
looks like the final result, while the original
technique is still unable to produce anything

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.510
usable.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.790
That is absolutely insane.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.570
Okay, so it’s quick.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:09.570
Real quick.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.320
But how good are the results?

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:19.630
Well, the previous technique was able to produce
this after approximately 1.5 days of training.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.400
And, what about the new technique?

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.340
All it needs is 8.8.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.220
8.8 what?

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.220
Days?

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.220
Hours?

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.940
No-no. 8.8 minutes.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.470
And the result looks like this.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:40.580
Not only as good, but even a bit better than
what the previous method could do in 1.5 days.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:41.580
Whoa.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:47.660
So, I mentioned that the results are typically
even a bit better, which is quite remarkable.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.910
Let’s take a closer look.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:57.640
This is the previous technique after more
than a day, this is the new method after 18

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.640
minutes.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.700
Now, it says that the new technique is 0.3
decibels better, that does not sound like

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.290
much, does it?

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:12.500
Well, note that the decibel scale is not linear,
it is logarithmic.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:13.660
What does this mean?

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:14.660
It means this!

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.660
Look.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.819
A small numerical difference in the numbers
can mean mean a big difference in quality.

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:23.920
And, it is really close to the real results.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.140
And all this after 20 minutes of processing?

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.140
Bravo!

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.160
And, it does not stop there.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.990
The technique is also quite robust.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:39.970
It works well on forward-facing scenes, 360
degree rotations, and we can even use it to

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:45.130
disassemble a scene into its foreground and
background elements.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:50.610
Note that the previous NERF technique we compared
to was published just about a year and a half

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:51.610
ago.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.920
Such incredible improvement in so little time.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:01.330
And here comes the kicker: all this is possible
today with a handcrafted technique, no AI

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.330
is required.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:13.760
What a time 

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:46.800
to be alive!

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:55.259
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

